yblue\"></rect><rect x=\"110\" y=\"210\" width=\"37.5\" height=\"40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"deepskyblue\"></rect></svg><figcaption>Derivative of ln x.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"::: proof\nFor ``x>0`` and ``h>0``, ``\\ln\\left(x+ h\\right)-\\ln(x)`` is the area of the plane region bounded by ``y=\\frac{1}{t}``, ``y=0``, and the vertical lines ``t=x`` and ``t=x+h``; it is the shaded area in the next figure.\n\nComparing this area with that of two rectangles, we see that\n```math\n\\frac{h}{x+h}<\\textrm{shaded area}=\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x<\\frac{h}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nHence, the Newton quotient for ``\\ln x`` satisfies\n```math\n\\frac{1}{x+h}<\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}<\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nLetting ``h`` approach ``0`` from the right, we obtain (by the Squeeze Theorem applied to one-sided limits)\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0^+}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nA similar argument shows that if ``0<x+h<x``, then\n```math\n\\frac{1}{x}<\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}<\\frac{1}{x+h}\\,,\n```\nso that\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0^-}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nCombining these two one-sided limits we get the desired result:\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nThe two properties ``\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}`` and ``\\ln 1=0`` are sufficient to determine the function ``\\ln x`` completely. We can deduce from these two properties that ``\\ln x`` satisfies the appropriate laws of logarithms:\n\n::: proposition\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln\\left(xy\\right)=\\ln x+\\ln y&&\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\ln x\\\\\n&\\ln\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\ln x-\\ln y&&\\ln\\left(x^r\\right)=r\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nBecause we do not want to assume that exponentials are continuous, we should regard the last property for the moment as only valid for exponents ``r`` that are rational numbers.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nWe will only prove the first property because the other parts are proved by the same method. If ``y>0`` is a constant, then by the Chain Rule,\n```math\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x\\right)=\\frac{y}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x}=0\n```\nfor all ``x>0``. So, ``\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x=C`` (a constant). Putting ``x=1``, we get ``C=\\ln y`` and the first property follows.\n:::\n\nThe last property shows that ``\\ln\\left(2^n\\right)=n\\ln 2\\to\\infty`` as ``n\\to\\infty``. Therefore, we also have ``\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n=-n\\ln 2\\to-\\infty`` as ``n\\to\\infty``. Since ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}>0`` for ``x>0``, it follows that ``\\ln x`` is increasing, so we must have\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\ln x=\\infty\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\ln x=-\\infty\\,.\n```\n\n!!! example \"Show that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}`` for any ``x\\ne 0``. Hence find ``\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x``.\"\n\n\tIf ``x>0``, then\n\t```math\n\t\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tIf ``x<0``, then\n\t```math\n\t\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{-x}\\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tTherefore, ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}``, and on any interval not containing ``x=0``,\n\t```math\n\t\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x=\\ln\\left|x\\right|+C\\,.\n\t```\n\n### The Exponential Function\n\nThe function ``\\ln x`` is bijective on its domain, the interval ``\\left]0,\\infty\\right[``, so it has an inverse there. For the moment, let us call this inverse ``\\exp x``. Thus,\n```math\n\\forall y>0:x=\\ln y\\implies y=\\exp x\\,.\n```\n\nSince ``\\ln 1=0``, we have ``\\exp 0=1``. The domain of ``\\mathrm{exp}`` is ``\\left]-\\infty,\\infty\\right[``, the range of ``\\mathrm{ln}``. The range of ``\\mathrm{exp}`` is ``\\left]0,\\infty\\right[``, the domain of ``\\mathrm{ln}``. We have cancellation identities\n```math\n\\forall x\\in \\mathbb{R}:\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=x\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\forall x>0:\\exp\\left(\\ln x\\right)=x\\,.\n```\n\nWe can deduce various properties of ``\\exp`` from corresponding properties of ``\\ln``. Not surprisingly, they are properties we would expect an exponential function to have.\n\n::: proposition\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)&&\\exp\\left(x+y\\right)=\\exp x\\exp y\\\\\n&\\exp\\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\exp x}&&\\exp\\left(x-y\\right)=\\frac{\\exp x}{\\exp y}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n:::\n\nFor the moment, the first property is asserted only for rational numbers ``r``.\n\n::: proof\n\nWe prove only the first property, the rest are done similarly.\n\nIf ``u=\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r``, then, ``\\ln u=r\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=rx``. Therefore, ``u=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)``.\n:::\n\nNow we make an important definition!\n\n::: definition\nLet ``\\mathcal{e}=\\exp\\left(1\\right)``.\n:::\n\nThe number ``\\mathcal{e}`` satisfies ``\\ln \\mathcal{e} = 1``, so the area bounded by the curve ``y=\\frac{1}{t}``, the ``t``-axis, and the vertical lines ``t=1`` and ``t=\\mathcal{e}`` must be equal to ``1`` square unit.","output":"","result":"::: proof\nFor ``x>0`` and ``h>0``, ``\\ln\\left(x+ h\\right)-\\ln(x)`` is the area of the plane region bounded by ``y=\\frac{1}{t}``, ``y=0``, and the vertical lines ``t=x`` and ``t=x+h``; it is the shaded area in the next figure.\n\nComparing this area with that of two rectangles, we see that\n```math\n\\frac{h}{x+h}<\\textrm{shaded area}=\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x<\\frac{h}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nHence, the Newton quotient for ``\\ln x`` satisfies\n```math\n\\frac{1}{x+h}<\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}<\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nLetting ``h`` approach ``0`` from the right, we obtain (by the Squeeze Theorem applied to one-sided limits)\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0^+}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nA similar argument shows that if ``0<x+h<x``, then\n```math\n\\frac{1}{x}<\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}<\\frac{1}{x+h}\\,,\n```\nso that\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0^-}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n\nCombining these two one-sided limits we get the desired result:\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nThe two properties ``\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}`` and ``\\ln 1=0`` are sufficient to determine the function ``\\ln x`` completely. We can deduce from these two properties that ``\\ln x`` satisfies the appropriate laws of logarithms:\n\n::: proposition\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln\\left(xy\\right)=\\ln x+\\ln y&&\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\ln x\\\\\n&\\ln\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\ln x-\\ln y&&\\ln\\left(x^r\\right)=r\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nBecause we do not want to assume that exponentials are continuous, we should regard the last property for the moment as only valid for exponents ``r`` that are rational numbers.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nWe will only prove the first property because the other parts are proved by the same method. If ``y>0`` is a constant, then by the Chain Rule,\n```math\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x\\right)=\\frac{y}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x}=0\n```\nfor all ``x>0``. So, ``\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x=C`` (a constant). Putting ``x=1``, we get ``C=\\ln y`` and the first property follows.\n:::\n\nThe last property shows that ``\\ln\\left(2^n\\right)=n\\ln 2\\to\\infty`` as ``n\\to\\infty``. Therefore, we also have ``\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n=-n\\ln 2\\to-\\infty`` as ``n\\to\\infty``. Since ``\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}>0`` for ``x>0``, it follows that ``\\ln x`` is increasing, so we must have\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\ln x=\\infty\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\ln x=-\\infty\\,.\n```\n\n!!! example \"Show that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}`` for any ``x\\ne 0``. Hence find ``\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x``.\"\n\n\tIf ``x>0``, then\n\t```math\n\t\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tIf ``x<0``, then\n\t```math\n\t\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{-x}\\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tTherefore, ``\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}``, and on any interval not containing ``x=0``,\n\t```math\n\t\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x=\\ln\\left|x\\right|+C\\,.\n\t```\n\n### The Exponential Function\n\nThe function ``\\ln x`` is bijective on its domain, the interval ``\\left]0,\\infty\\right[``, so it has an inverse there. For the moment, let us call this inverse ``\\exp x``. Thus,\n```math\n\\forall y>0:x=\\ln y\\implies y=\\exp x\\,.\n```\n\nSince ``\\ln 1=0``, we have ``\\exp 0=1``. The domain of ``\\mathrm{exp}`` is ``\\left]-\\infty,\\infty\\right[``, the range of ``\\mathrm{ln}``. The range of ``\\mathrm{exp}`` is ``\\left]0,\\infty\\right[``, the domain of ``\\mathrm{ln}``. We have cancellation identities\n```math\n\\forall x\\in \\mathbb{R}:\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=x\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\forall x>0:\\exp\\left(\\ln x\\right)=x\\,.\n```\n\nWe can deduce various properties of ``\\exp`` from corresponding properties of ``\\ln``. Not surprisingly, they are properties we would expect an exponential function to have.\n\n::: proposition\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)&&\\exp\\left(x+y\\right)=\\exp x\\exp y\\\\\n&\\exp\\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\exp x}&&\\exp\\left(x-y\\right)=\\frac{\\exp x}{\\exp y}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n:::\n\nFor the moment, the first property is asserted only for rational numbers ``r``.\n\n::: proof\n\nWe prove only the first property, the rest are done similarly.\n\nIf ``u=\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r``, then, ``\\ln u=r\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=rx``. Therefore, ``u=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)``.\n:::\n\nNow we make an important definition!\n\n::: definition\nLet ``\\mathcal{e}=\\exp\\left(1\\right)``.\n:::\n\nThe number ``\\mathcal{e}`` satisfies ``\\ln \\mathcal{e} = 1``, so the area bounded by the curve ``y=\\frac{1}{t}``, the ``t``-axis, and the vertical lines ``t=1`` and ``t=\\mathcal{e}`` must be equal to ``1`` square unit.","resultHTML":"<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&gt;0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h&gt;0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln\\left(x+ h\\right)-\\ln(x)\\)</span> is the area of the plane region bounded by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=\\frac{1}{t}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=0\\)</span>, and the vertical lines <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=x+h\\)</span>; it is the shaded area in the next figure.</p>\n<p>Comparing this area with that of two rectangles, we see that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{h}{x+h}&lt;\\textrm{shaded area}=\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x&lt;\\frac{h}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Hence, the Newton quotient for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln x\\)</span> satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x+h}&lt;\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}&lt;\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Letting <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h\\)</span> approach <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> from the right, we obtain (by the Squeeze Theorem applied to one-sided limits)</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{h\\to0^+}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A similar argument shows that if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;x+h&lt;x\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x}&lt;\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}&lt;\\frac{1}{x+h}\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>so that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{h\\to0^-}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Combining these two one-sided limits we get the desired result:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\ln\\left(x+h\\right)-\\ln x}{h}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The two properties <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln 1=0\\)</span> are sufficient to determine the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln x\\)</span> completely. We can deduce from these two properties that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln x\\)</span> satisfies the appropriate laws of logarithms:</p>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\ln\\left(xy\\right)=\\ln x+\\ln y&amp;&amp;\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\ln x\\\\\n&amp;\\ln\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\ln x-\\ln y&amp;&amp;\\ln\\left(x^r\\right)=r\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Because we do not want to assume that exponentials are continuous, we should regard the last property for the moment as only valid for exponents <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> that are rational numbers.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>We will only prove the first property because the other parts are proved by the same method. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y&gt;0\\)</span> is a constant, then by the Chain Rule,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x\\right)=\\frac{y}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x}=0\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&gt;0\\)</span>. So, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln\\left(xy\\right)-\\ln x=C\\)</span> (a constant). Putting <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>, we get <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C=\\ln y\\)</span> and the first property follows.</p>\n</div>\n<p>The last property shows that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln\\left(2^n\\right)=n\\ln 2\\to\\infty\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)</span>. Therefore, we also have <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n=-n\\ln 2\\to-\\infty\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}&gt;0\\)</span> for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&gt;0\\)</span>, it follows that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln x\\)</span> is increasing, so we must have</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\ln x=\\infty\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\ln x=-\\infty\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}\\)</span> for any <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\ne 0\\)</span>. Hence find <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&gt;0\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln x=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&lt;0\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{-x}\\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Therefore, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\hphantom{x}}{\\mathrm{d} x}\\ln \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{x}\\)</span>, and on any interval not containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=0\\)</span>,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d} x=\\ln\\left|x\\right|+C\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</aside>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-ES111_051Lecture05-c3644-the-exponential-function\">\n<h1>The Exponential Function</h1>\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln x\\)</span> is bijective on its domain, the interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]0,\\infty\\right[\\)</span>, so it has an inverse there. For the moment, let us call this inverse <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\exp x\\)</span>. Thus,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall y&gt;0:x=\\ln y\\implies y=\\exp x\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln 1=0\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\exp 0=1\\)</span>. The domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{exp}\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]-\\infty,\\infty\\right[\\)</span>, the range of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{ln}\\)</span>. The range of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{exp}\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]0,\\infty\\right[\\)</span>, the domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{ln}\\)</span>. We have cancellation identities</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall x\\in \\mathbb{R}:\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=x\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\forall x&gt;0:\\exp\\left(\\ln x\\right)=x\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We can deduce various properties of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\exp\\)</span> from corresponding properties of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln\\)</span>. Not surprisingly, they are properties we would expect an exponential function to have.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)&amp;&amp;\\exp\\left(x+y\\right)=\\exp x\\exp y\\\\\n&amp;\\exp\\left(-x\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\exp x}&amp;&amp;\\exp\\left(x-y\\right)=\\frac{\\exp x}{\\exp y}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>For the moment, the first property is asserted only for rational numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>We prove only the first property, the rest are done similarly.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=\\left(\\exp x\\right)^r\\)</span>, then, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln u=r\\ln\\left(\\exp x\\right)=rx\\)</span>. Therefore, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=\\exp\\left(rx\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Now we make an important definition!</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal{e}=\\exp\\left(1\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>The number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal{e}\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ln \\mathcal{e} = 1\\)</span>, so the area bounded by the curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=\\frac{1}{t}\\)</span>, the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>-axis, and the vertical lines <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=\\mathcal{e}\\)</span> must be equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span> square unit.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 50;\nconst fontX = 14;\nconst fontY = 18;\nconst xmid = 0.5 * scale;\nconst ymid = 5 * scale;\nconst e = Math.E;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 5.5 * scale,\n    height: 5.5 * scale,\n    caption: String.raw`The definition of \\(\\mathcal{e}\\).`,\n});\n\nconst points = [[xmid + scale, ymid]];\nconst steps = Math.ceil((e - 1) / 0.01);\n\nfor (let i = 0; i <= steps; i++) {\n    const t = 1 + (e - 1) * i / steps;\n    points.push([xmid + t * scale, ymid - scale / t]);\n}\npoints.push([xmid + e * scale, ymid]);\n\nfigure.polygon()\n    .points(points.map(([px, py]) => `${px},${py}`).join(\" \"))\n    .fill(\"lightblue\")\n    .stroke(\"none\");\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [xmid, ymid],\n    scale,\n    x: {\n        ticks: [\n            1,\n            { value: e, label: String.raw`\\mathcal{e}` },\n        ],\n    },\n    y: {\n        ticks: [\n            {\n                value: 1 / e,\n                label: String.raw`\\frac{1}{\\mathcal{e}}`,\n                length: 1,\n            },\n            1,\n        ],\n    },\n});\n\n// axes() currently uses \"x\" as its horizontal-axis symbol.\nfigure.element().querySelector(\"foreignObject div\").textContent =\n    String.raw`\\(t\\)`;\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n    x: xmid - fontX - 2,\n    y: ymid,\n    width: fontX,\n    height: fontY,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure.plot((t) => 1 / t, {\n    domain: [0.01, 5],\n    samples: 500,\n    width: 1,\n    stroke: \"red\",\n});\n\nfor (const t of [1, e]) {\n    figure.point(t, 1 / t, {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"red\",\n        stroke: \"red\",\n    });\n}\n\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\textrm{Area}=1`, {\n    x: xmid + 1.25 * scale,\n    y: ymid - 0.35 * scale,\n    width: 4 * fontX,\n    height: fontY,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"ty1ℯ1ℯ1OArea=1The definition of ℯ.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"275\" height=\"275\" viewBox=\"0 0 275 275\"><polygon points=\"75,250 75,200 75.4995005315288,200.49455987313868 75.99900106305759,200.9794319910525 76.49850159458637,201.45489824770348 76.99800212611518,201.92122970539256 77.49750265764396,202.3786871100623 77.99700318917274,202.8275213774588 78.49650372070155,203.267974052058 78.99600425223034,203.70027774051937 79.49550478375913,204.12465652130163 79.99500531528793,204.54132633195636 80.49450584681671,204.95049533550525 80.9940063783455,205.35236426720803 81.4935069098743,205.74712676293365 81.99300744140308,206.1349696702643 82.49250797293189,206.51607334338192 82.99200850446067,206.89061192271512 83.49150903598947,207.25875360025734 83.99100956751826,207.62066087140582 84.49051009904706,207.97649077411347 84.99001063057584,208.32639511609295 85.48951116210463,208.67052069076405 85.98901169363342,209.00900948258894 86.48851222516222,209.34199886239963 86.988012756691,209.6696217732815 87.4875132882198,209.9920069075416 87.9870138197486,210.3092788752566 88.48651435127739,210.62155836486403 88.98601488280617,210.9289622962317 89.48551541433497,211.2316039666133 89.98501594586376,211.52959318987251 90.48451647739255,211.82303642933542 90.98401700892134,2