07264 316.0731788254996,26.5461356885461 316.5718443260694,26.42549808535663 317.07050982663924,26.309691997854692 317.569175327209,26.198728944818782 318.06784082777887,26.092619963309716 318.5665063283487,25.991375607572806 319.06517182891844,25.89500594798806 319.56383732948825,25.803520570068514 320.06250283005807,25.716928573506856 320.5611683306279,25.635238571270214 321.0598338311977,25.55845868874357 321.5584993317675,25.486596562921484 322.0571648323373,25.41965934164849 322.55583033290714,25.357653682908136 323.05449583347695,25.300585754160743 323.55316133404676,25.248461231729927 324.0518268346166,25.201285300238048 324.5504923351864,25.159062652090455 325.04915783575615,25.12179748700879 325.54782333632596,25.08949351161324 326.0464888368958,25.062153939053886 326.5451543374656,25.039781488691034 327.0438198380354,25.022378385824837 327.54248533860516,25.00994636147383 328.04115083917503,25.002486652202847 328.53981633974485,25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"red\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline><circle cx=\"171.46018366025515\" cy=\"125\" r=\"3\" fill=\"red\" stroke=\"red\"></circle><circle cx=\"328.53981633974485\" cy=\"25\" r=\"3\" fill=\"red\" stroke=\"red\"></circle></svg><figcaption>The graph of <math><mrow><mi>Sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"Being bijective, ``\\operatorname{Sin}`` has an inverse function which is denoted ``\\operatorname{Arcsin}`` (or, in some books and computer programs, by ``\\operatorname{arcsin}``,  ``\\operatorname{asin}``, or ``\\operatorname{Sin}^{-1}``) and which is called the *inverse sine* or *arcsine function*.\n\n::: definition\n```math\n\\forall y\\in\\left[-\\frac{\\uppi}{2},\\frac{\\uppi}{2}\\right]: x=\\sin y=\\operatorname{Sin}y\\implies y = \\operatorname{Arcsin} x\\,.\n```\n:::","output":"","result":"Being bijective, ``\\operatorname{Sin}`` has an inverse function which is denoted ``\\operatorname{Arcsin}`` (or, in some books and computer programs, by ``\\operatorname{arcsin}``,  ``\\operatorname{asin}``, or ``\\operatorname{Sin}^{-1}``) and which is called the *inverse sine* or *arcsine function*.\n\n::: definition\n```math\n\\forall y\\in\\left[-\\frac{\\uppi}{2},\\frac{\\uppi}{2}\\right]: x=\\sin y=\\operatorname{Sin}y\\implies y = \\operatorname{Arcsin} x\\,.\n```\n:::","resultHTML":"<p>Being bijective, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{Sin}\\)</span> has an inverse function which is denoted <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{Arcsin}\\)</span> (or, in some books and computer programs, by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{arcsin}\\)</span>,  <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{asin}\\)</span>, or <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{Sin}^{-1}\\)</span>) and which is called the <em>inverse sine</em> or <em>arcsine function</em>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall y\\in\\left[-\\frac{\\uppi}{2},\\frac{\\uppi}{2}\\right]: x=\\sin y=\\operatorname{Sin}y\\implies y = \\operatorname{Arcsin} x\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 50;\nconst halfPi = Math.PI / 2;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 3 * scale,\n    height: 4 * scale,\n    caption: String.raw`The graph of \\(\\operatorname{Arcsin}x\\).`,\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [1.5 * scale, 2 * scale],\n    scale,\n    x: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n    y: {\n        ticks: [\n            {\n                value: -halfPi,\n                label: String.raw`-\\frac{\\uppi}{2}`,\n                length: 2,\n            },\n            {\n                value: halfPi,\n                label: String.raw`\\frac{\\uppi}{2}`,\n                length: 1,\n            },\n        ],\n    },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(Math.asin, {\n    domain: [-1, 1],\n    samples: 201,\n    width: 1,\n    stroke: \"red\",\n});\n\nfor (const x of [-1, 1]) {\n    figure.point(x, Math.asin(x), {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"red\",\n        stroke: \"red\",\n    });\n}\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−11−π2π2The graph of Arcsin⁡x.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"150\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 150 200\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"150,100 143,96 143,104\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"75,0 71,7 79,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"136\" y=\"70\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"83\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"104\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"9\" y=\"108\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"104\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"109\" y=\"108\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"71\" y1=\"178.53981633974485\" x2=\"79\" y2=\"178.53981633974485\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"39\" y=\"162.53981633974485\" width=\"28\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: